WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.440
Starship Flight 15 caught ship 42 after 14 orbits. The first orbital return to the tower. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 163. This is a special on Flight 15. Pad 2 to the arms, why the ship catch is a different problem from a super heavy catch, and an honest score of what still has to be shown. Picture the last minute, not the launch. Ship 42 is already through the fire. It has left orbit, come through reentry,

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:33.720
and is on a banking approach toward the same Texas tower it left the day before.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.160
Three Raptor engines light for the flip and the slowdown. Near hover the stack

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:44.360
sheds to one engine. The tower arms, Meccazilla in SpaceX's own language,

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.040
close on the fittings. That is the moment this flight is about, not a splash

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.520
down, not a landing on legs. A spacecraft that had been in orbit caught by its

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:02.100
launch tower. The catch happened on November 14th, 2026, at about 3.21pm central at Starbase.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.980
A super heavy booster catch is not this. SpaceX had already caught super heavy boosters

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.020
on earlier flights, and those boosters come home from a suborbital hop.

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:18.760
Ship 42 came home from orbit. The distinction is the engineering story. Name the first for

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:23.620
exactly what it is. The ship was caught from orbit. The super heavy booster on this flight

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:29.320
was not recovered. Catching both stages on one flight is still ahead, a caught ship flying

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:37.113
again is still ahead. Starship Flight 15 lifted off from Pad 2 at Starbase Texas on November 13th,

00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:46.753
2026 at about 4.47 pm central 2247 UTC. The stack was super heavy booster 22 under ship 42.

00:01:47.433 --> 00:01:53.553
The payload was 26 starlink version 3 satellites. The first operational version 3 batch flown on

00:01:53.553 --> 00:01:58.833
Starship. The planned profile was the one the program has been building toward. Super

00:01:58.833 --> 00:02:03.813
heavy booster return to the tower, ship to orbit, deploy, then bring the ship home

00:02:03.813 --> 00:02:09.813
to the same tower. That dual-catch plan did not hold. Ship 42 reached orbit, deployed

00:02:09.813 --> 00:02:15.093
its satellites, and flew a mission lasting about 22 and a half hours. A Thursday afternoon

00:02:15.093 --> 00:02:20.013
launch to a Friday afternoon catch, a full working day in space before the arms closed.

00:02:20.013 --> 00:02:22.013
post. SpaceX posted, quote,

00:02:22.353 --> 00:02:24.393
mechazilla catches Starship, end quote.

00:02:24.993 --> 00:02:29.893
No detailed post-catch heat shield or structural inspection results had been published as of November

00:02:30.693 --> 00:02:31.293
15th.

00:02:31.333 --> 00:02:34.173
No Starship vehicle that has been to orbit has flown again.

00:02:35.335 --> 00:02:38.495
Lift off from pad 2 afternoon of November 13th.

00:02:38.915 --> 00:02:42.275
Super Heavy Booster 22 and Ship 42 climbed together.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.755
Staging happens on the usual ascent.

00:02:45.175 --> 00:02:49.375
Then the Super Heavy Boosters return, which is where this flight diverges from the plan.

00:02:49.375 --> 00:02:54.835
The boostback burn is the burn that turns a super heavy booster around and aims it at the tower.

00:02:55.335 --> 00:03:01.235
That burn ended early. Reporting, including SpaceX's own account as relayed by outlets covering

00:03:01.235 --> 00:03:07.355
the flight, ties that to a low fuel mass alarm. The root cause was still under investigation two

00:03:07.355 --> 00:03:12.775
days later. Elon Musk pointed to a fuel and performance issue he described as fixable with

00:03:12.775 --> 00:03:17.915
engine and software updates, with a possible contribution from minor engine underperformance.

00:03:17.915 --> 00:03:18.055
clearance.

00:03:18.655 --> 00:03:20.735
What the vehicle did next is the part to keep.

00:03:21.375 --> 00:03:27.035
About 6 minutes and 35 seconds after liftoff, Super Heavy Booster 22's guidance, navigation

00:03:27.035 --> 00:03:31.875
and control system, the onboard brains that know where the booster is and whether it

00:03:31.875 --> 00:03:36.755
catches safe, judged it was not in a safe position for the tower and triggered an automated

00:03:36.755 --> 00:03:40.095
abort to an offshore contingency site in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:03:40.775 --> 00:03:45.715
It performed a landing burn, cut the engines off while hovering, then the flight termination

00:03:45.715 --> 00:03:50.335
system activated on splashdown. That is not a catch of the super heavy booster.

00:03:50.935 --> 00:03:56.175
It is also not a random failure of the catch hardware. It is a vehicle refusing a catch at judged

00:03:56.175 --> 00:04:01.995
unsafe. SpaceX has described that aboard as the system correctly prioritizing pad safety.

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:08.555
Ship 42 is already on its own. Deployment of the Starlink version 3 satellites begins about 35

00:04:08.555 --> 00:04:15.195
minutes after liftoff, into an orbit around 275 kilometers, low-earth orbit, a few hundred

00:04:15.195 --> 00:04:19.895
kilometers up, close enough that the air is still a faint drag and the satellites immediately

00:04:19.895 --> 00:04:25.975
start raising themselves. Contact is confirmed. The ship then settles into the long, quiet middle

00:04:25.975 --> 00:04:30.735
of the mission. The decision to bring the ship home is not automatic in the way of pre-written

00:04:30.735 --> 00:04:36.195
timeline sounds. Before reentry, the heat shield was inspected from orbit by camera equipped

00:04:36.195 --> 00:04:41.915
starling satellites and reported in good posture, good shape for return. That inspection

00:04:41.915 --> 00:04:48.275
is what made a catch attempt the plan instead of a backup splashdown. Around 2023 UTC on

00:04:48.275 --> 00:04:54.775
November 14th, Ship 42 does the deorbit burn. Reentry follows, then the sequence the catch

00:04:54.775 --> 00:05:00.615
actually requires, a banking approach, three raptors for the landing flip, and the slow descent,

00:05:01.215 --> 00:05:05.455
reduction to one engine near hover, and the tower arms closing on the fittings.

00:05:05.455 --> 00:05:08.235
The super heavy booster is already in the gulf.

00:05:08.695 --> 00:05:09.835
The ship is in the arms.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:15.030
From an engineering standpoint, catching the ship is not a super heavy catch with a new

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:15.670
coat of paint.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.970
They differ in kind, and the difference starts with energy.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.590
A super heavy booster never reaches orbit.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:27.550
It does a suborbital hop, it lifts the stack, separates, turns around, and comes home

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:30.550
from a trajectory that is fast and high but not orbital.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:32.510
The ship does reach orbit.

00:05:32.510 --> 00:05:37.470
Orbital speed is a different regime. To stay in orbit is to be in continuous free

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:42.790
fall around the Earth. To leave orbit and come home, that speed has to go somewhere. The

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:48.130
energy does not vanish. It becomes heat. That is the heat shield's job. During reentry,

00:05:48.310 --> 00:05:53.570
the ship is a blunt body punching into thickening air. Kinetic energy converts to thermal energy

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:58.610
in the shock layer. The superheated air piled up in front of the vehicle. The tiles have

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:03.030
have to take that load, keep it out of the structure, and still leave the vehicle controllable.

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:08.090
A super heavy booster returning from a hop has a heat problem too, but not this one.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:13.250
The ship has to shed orbital energy and arrive at a tower in Texas as a vehicle you are still

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:14.210
willing to catch.

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:19.550
Before Flight 15, SpaceX could not inspect an orbital starship heat shield on the ground

00:06:19.550 --> 00:06:20.830
after a real reentry.

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.630
After the catch, that hardware is on the ground.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:26.570
The evidence value is still the fact of the recovery.

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.930
The first orbital heat shield this program can walk up to.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.650
Control through reentry is the next problem.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:38.190
Plasma, dynamic pressure, the force of the air slamming into the vehicle, and shifting

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.570
mass properties all eat control authority.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:45.450
The vehicle has to hold attitude through a corridor that is unforgiving in two directions.

00:06:46.150 --> 00:06:48.170
Two steep and the heating and load spike.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.270
Two shallow and you skip or miss the landing zone.

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:55.870
Then, at the bottom of that corridor, you must re-light engines that have been coasting

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:01.270
in space, flip a body that was flying as a belly-first re-entry vehicle, and change it into

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:02.230
something that can hover.

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:06.930
The flip is not a flourish, a ship does not have landing legs in this architecture.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:08.530
It is meant to be caught.

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:12.210
That means the final trajectory is not land somewhere in its own.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:17.570
It is arrive, almost hovering, at a pair of arms, with fittings the arms can close

00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.390
ozone, with almost no margin in position, velocity, or attitude.

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:24.950
Reporting on flight 15 describes a banking approach.

00:07:25.490 --> 00:07:29.210
Three raptors for the flip and the slowdown, then one engine near hover.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:34.670
That engine out logic is how you go from kill leftover orbital class energy to be catchable.

00:07:35.230 --> 00:07:36.590
The arms do the last inches.

00:07:37.150 --> 00:07:41.070
If you are high, long, fast, or tilted, you do not catch.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.370
You abort to water or you fail.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:44.890
Why do this at all?

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:49.090
Because landing legs are mass you fly to orbit and never get back as payload.

00:07:49.630 --> 00:07:51.950
A tower catch lets the ship omit that gear.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:57.130
Less dry mass, the structure you have to carry even when the tanks are empty, means more

00:07:57.130 --> 00:07:59.670
payload or more propellant margin or both.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:03.050
Catching at the tower also means you are already at the pad.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:09.490
The path to a fast restack, super heavy booster or ship lord, inspected, restacked, flown

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.490
again, runs through this hardware.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:16.810
A splash down, even a gentle one, means fishing a vehicle out of salt water and losing the

00:08:16.810 --> 00:08:17.630
cadence argument.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:23.610
SpaceX wants tower catches because the architecture of reuse, as they have designed it, has no

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:25.050
legs and no spare hours.

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:27.270
The honest comparison is simple.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:31.890
Catching the super heavy booster was the proof that mechazilla works on a returning stage

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:33.050
that never went orbital.

00:08:33.670 --> 00:08:38.530
Catching the ship is the proof that the same idea survives orbital energy, a heat shield,

00:08:38.850 --> 00:08:39.930
a relight, and a flip.

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:46.122
flight 15 did the second of those. It did not do both on the same flight. None of that

00:08:46.122 --> 00:08:51.362
architecture arrived on flight 15 as a new invention. Those earlier super heavy booster

00:08:51.362 --> 00:08:56.382
catches proved that a returning stage can be guided to the tower, that the arms can close

00:08:56.382 --> 00:09:01.482
on fittings, and that the pad can be kept clear enough for the attempt. Each of them made

00:09:01.482 --> 00:09:06.682
the next attempt more plausible, because the tower, the software, and the abort logic had

00:09:06.682 --> 00:09:08.542
been exercised on a real vehicle.

00:09:09.162 --> 00:09:10.982
What they did not prove is the ship.

00:09:11.642 --> 00:09:15.142
A super heavy booster's return is a high energy landing from a hop.

00:09:15.802 --> 00:09:18.982
The ship's return is reentry from orbit plus a landing.

00:09:19.582 --> 00:09:25.722
Until this flight, that chain, deorbit, heat shield, control, relight, flip, arms, had

00:09:25.722 --> 00:09:26.802
not ended in a catch.

00:09:27.522 --> 00:09:31.942
Independent coverage treats the ship catch as the milestone that validates return to

00:09:31.942 --> 00:09:34.042
launch site for an orbital upper stage.

00:09:34.042 --> 00:09:38.222
Bring the vehicle all the way home to the pad at left, instead of to water.

00:09:38.882 --> 00:09:43.562
That is the correct framing, not that the tower is new, but that the orbital half of

00:09:43.562 --> 00:09:45.082
the vehicle has now used it.

00:09:45.602 --> 00:09:50.062
The research available on November 15th does not give a detailed public test campaign

00:09:50.062 --> 00:09:55.482
history for super heavy booster 22 or ship 42 beyond their roles on this flight.

00:09:56.042 --> 00:09:58.202
What it does give is the in-flight evidence.

00:09:58.202 --> 00:10:03.282
ship 42's heat shield was imaged in orbit and judged in good shape for a return,

00:10:03.802 --> 00:10:08.642
which is why the catch was attempted. Super Heavy Booster 22's guidance, navigation,

00:10:09.062 --> 00:10:12.082
and control aborted the Super Heavy catch on its own judgment.

00:10:12.762 --> 00:10:16.422
Gwynne shot well before the flight had pointed to FAA licensing support

00:10:16.742 --> 00:10:19.202
for return to launch site and catch operations.

00:10:19.782 --> 00:10:23.362
The license is part of the road here too. You cannot attempt this catch

00:10:23.362 --> 00:10:26.622
without a regulator willing to accept a returning orbital stage

00:10:26.622 --> 00:10:27.902
aimed at a tower in Texas.

00:10:28.602 --> 00:10:30.862
One more thing the road does not yet include.

00:10:31.482 --> 00:10:35.502
Musk has previously talked about potential reflight timelines in general terms.

00:10:36.002 --> 00:10:38.102
Nothing has been confirmed for ship 42.

00:10:38.782 --> 00:10:40.222
Treat that as unconfirmed.

00:10:40.602 --> 00:10:42.822
The program has now caught a ship from orbit.

00:10:43.322 --> 00:10:44.542
It has not reused one.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:49.580
Full reuse of both stages is the cost and cadence argument for Starship.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:52.760
Catching the ship is a necessary piece of that argument.

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:54.540
It is not the whole argument.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:59.780
Until a super heavy booster and a ship are both caught on one flight, and until a caught

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:05.420
ship flies again, and until that loop is short, the vehicle is not a fully reusable rocket.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:11.280
Company language about progress toward full reusability should be heard as progress, not

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:11.900
as arrival.

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:17.020
Musk, after this flight, said the super heavy booster issue does not change the timeline

00:11:17.020 --> 00:11:18.920
to high-volume reusable flights.

00:11:19.480 --> 00:11:22.220
That is his projection, not a demonstrated cadence.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:26.360
Starlink version 3 is the near-term operational payload.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:31.240
If Starship is going to be the version 3 workhorse, two things have to be true.

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:36.480
The upper stage has to deliver the satellites, and the upper stage has to come home in

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:41.280
a condition that makes the next flight a factory problem rather than a construction problem.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:43.580
The catch is evidence for the second.

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:45.640
It is not yet a production line.

00:11:45.640 --> 00:11:51.960
Caden still depends on inspection, on whether ship 42 is flyable again, and on whether the

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:55.500
super-heavy side of the pad can be caught as often as the plan assumes.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:12:00.640
Artemis sits further out, and the sources on this flight do not pretend otherwise.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:06.980
NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman congratulated SpaceX on the launch and the catch, and expressed

00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.800
interest in Starlink capability for Artemis III.

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:15.100
That is a statement about communications and capability, not a declaration that a lunar

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:16.020
or lander is ready.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:21.420
From first principles, a moon lander that needs orbital refueling needs many tanker flights,

00:12:21.940 --> 00:12:26.200
and tanker flights only get cheap if both stages are caught and flown again quickly.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.380
Flight 15 does not demonstrate that architecture.

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:34.320
It demonstrates that the ship half of a catch is possible after a real orbital mission.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.540
The refueling campaign still needs the rest of the loop.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:42.280
What the caught ship is worth as evidence is more specific than any of those features.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:48.000
Engineers can now inspect on the ground a heat shield that has been through orbital reentry

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:48.900
on a starship.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:53.260
They can look at tiles, at structure, at the fittings the arms grabbed.

00:12:53.920 --> 00:12:57.340
As of November 15th, those inspection results were not public.

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.680
The existence of the article on the ground is the news.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:06.120
What they find will decide whether this catch was a one-time success or the start of a

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:06.800
reuse line.

00:13:07.802 --> 00:13:12.702
One thing worth watching is how this flight gets scored because success on a test is

00:13:12.702 --> 00:13:19.162
not a single bin. Full success, as this attempt was drawn, was both stages caught, super heavy

00:13:19.162 --> 00:13:25.302
booster 22 in the arms, then ship 42 in the arms after orbit, with the payload deployed.

00:13:25.962 --> 00:13:31.882
That did not happen. The dual catch failed when super heavy booster 22 was diverted. Anyone

00:13:31.882 --> 00:13:36.482
calling this the first fully reusable rocket is getting ahead of the hardware. The next

00:13:36.482 --> 00:13:42.222
bin down is the one flight 15 actually occupies. The ship was caught from orbit, the payload

00:13:42.222 --> 00:13:47.782
was deployed. The super heavy booster was not recovered, but it aborted on its own guidance,

00:13:48.142 --> 00:13:52.402
navigation, and control logic rather than attempting an unsafe catch at the pad.

00:13:53.302 --> 00:13:59.082
SpaceX's description, the system prioritizing pad safety, is the right engineering read

00:13:59.082 --> 00:14:05.262
if the investigation confirms the fuel mass and position story. A vehicle that refuses a bad catch

00:14:05.262 --> 00:14:11.242
is the abort system working. It is also why the larger milestone waits. You cannot stack both

00:14:11.242 --> 00:14:16.802
stages reused on a super heavy booster that splashed down and was terminated. Below that,

00:14:16.982 --> 00:14:21.862
a ship that reenters and ditches to water. Still a heat shield data point, not a catch,

00:14:22.342 --> 00:14:28.542
not a rapid reuse path. Below that, a ship lost on reentry, or a catch attempt that damages

00:14:28.542 --> 00:14:33.102
the tower. Those would have been informative in the way destructive tests are informative.

00:14:33.722 --> 00:14:38.382
They would not have been the milestone. What went wrong as reported? The super heavy

00:14:38.382 --> 00:14:43.702
booster's boost back ended early on a low fuel mass alarm, possible minor engine under

00:14:43.702 --> 00:14:44.182
performance.

00:14:44.722 --> 00:14:50.062
The catch declared unsafe, divert to the gulf, landing burn with engines cut in a hover,

00:14:50.622 --> 00:14:52.202
flight termination on splashdown.

00:14:52.742 --> 00:14:53.922
What did not go wrong?

00:14:54.262 --> 00:14:58.922
The ship's orbital mission, the version 3 deployment, the pre-reentry heat shield check,

00:14:59.442 --> 00:15:02.302
the reentry, the flip, and the catch of ship 42.

00:15:02.942 --> 00:15:05.842
A test flight that fails informatively is still a test.

00:15:05.842 --> 00:15:10.842
The super heavy divert is informative. It does not get excused into a catch.

00:15:11.282 --> 00:15:13.942
The honest ledger, historic success on the ship,

00:15:14.442 --> 00:15:16.422
a missed recovery on the super heavy booster,

00:15:17.002 --> 00:15:19.062
pad intact, payload on orbit,

00:15:19.642 --> 00:15:21.702
reuse of an orbital ship still unproven.

00:15:22.647 --> 00:15:24.387
Here how the same events got named,

00:15:25.027 --> 00:15:27.107
SpaceX's own line was the blunt one.

00:15:27.487 --> 00:15:31.647
The company summary on its flight 15 page framed the ship catch as historic

00:15:31.647 --> 00:15:34.327
and as progress toward full reusability.

00:15:34.327 --> 00:15:39.587
Progress is the right word. Full reusability is not a fact this flight established.

00:15:40.207 --> 00:15:44.707
Musk's post-flight comments were the fuel and performance issue, the claim that engine

00:15:44.707 --> 00:15:49.587
and software updates can fix it, and the claim that the high-volume reusable timeline does

00:15:49.587 --> 00:15:54.467
not change. That last clause is a projection from him. Not a demonstration.

00:15:55.247 --> 00:16:00.367
Shotwell's relevant comments were pre-flight. FAA licensing support for return to launch

00:16:00.367 --> 00:16:05.927
site and catch work. Isaac Minne's congratulations tied his interest to Starlink for Artemis III.

00:16:06.687 --> 00:16:12.027
Hear that as NASA's administrator responding to a successful orbital catch and a Starlink deployment,

00:16:12.487 --> 00:16:18.087
not as an Artemis hardware milestone. Independent technical coverage, the space road,

00:16:18.407 --> 00:16:23.527
teslorati, NASA spaceflight, and others, has been largely aligned on the core events.

00:16:24.247 --> 00:16:29.087
The ship catch is treated as a major historic milestone for return to launch site of an orbital

00:16:29.087 --> 00:16:35.627
upper stage. The super heavy abort is treated as safety logic working correctly, not as a tower failure.

00:16:36.267 --> 00:16:40.927
Reporting describes the reentry, landing burn, and catch sequence as matching the plan.

00:16:41.607 --> 00:16:47.127
Three engines for the flip, reduction to one near hover. There is no serious public dispute in

00:16:47.127 --> 00:16:51.747
the sources about whether the ship was caught, or about whether the super heavy booster was.

00:16:52.387 --> 00:16:58.207
The argument, such as it is, is about what to name the result. The precise name is first catch of

00:16:58.207 --> 00:17:04.307
ship after an orbital mission. Super heavy booster not recovered. Both stage reuse still ahead.

00:17:05.243 --> 00:17:10.523
The next concrete milestones are on the ground first. Ship 42 is the first caught ship.

00:17:11.203 --> 00:17:17.483
Post-catch checks are expected before any reuse. As of November 15, SpaceX had not released official

00:17:17.483 --> 00:17:23.983
details on inspections, turnaround, or reflight. Any timeline for flying Ship 42 again is

00:17:23.983 --> 00:17:29.003
Unconfirmed. Watch for heat shield findings, structural findings at the catch fittings,

00:17:29.363 --> 00:17:33.603
